    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Old Austin Tales: Forgotten Austin Discotheques of the 1960s and 70s\ndate: 2025-06-09\n---\nOld Austin Tales: Forgotten Austin Discotheques of the 1960s and 70s\n\nSome folks around these parts like to think of Austin as \"The Live Music Capital of the World\", but as you probably know, Austin has quite a few dance clubs which don't play live music. What about them? Why should they be left out of the equation? I know modern dance music isn't for everyone, but for what they are, do our clubs just suck compared to those in other cities or something? Have they always sucked? Are they just a meat market? Is there an Austin version of Disco Stu? These questions and a few other mysteries are what we will be exploring today as the old time machine returns to the 1970s for a brief recharge. (three mile island cough cough)\n\nLet's start with a disclaimer. I'm an old person who remembers the days of Ohms and Paradox. I remember going to drag shows at Club 404, bondage night at Atomic Cafe, and hip-hop night at Nasty's. I'm 20+ years removed from \"the scene\" here, if there ever was such a thing. I have no idea what the club scene is like downtown or elsewhere in the city today. But until recently I also had no idea how it all began in this city, and that is probably a story worth sharing.\n\nMuch has been written and said about legendary local live music venues of the 20th century. The Armadillo, The (Original) Vulcan Gas Company, The New Orleans Club, Liberty Lunch, Victory Grill, and dozens of others. But what were the first discos in town? Few people seem to remember or talk about them at all. It was a little before my time, so I went back to the Statesman archive to look for whatever limited info there might be on the first discotheques.\n\nFirst allow me to wax pedantic a bit on the history of disco music and what a \"Discotheque\" is for some back story. Quoting the wikipedia article:\n\nThe term \"disco\" is shorthand for the word discoth\u00e8que, a French word for \"library of phonograph records\" derived from \"biblioth\u00e8que\". The word \"discoth\u00e8que\" had the same meaning in English in the 1950s.\n\n\"Disco"
