    "snippet": "---\ntitle: BELINDA'S BOOK NOTES\ndate: 2024-01-24\n---\nBook Notes for June-\n\nPoetry as a \"momentary stay against confusion.\"\n\nRobert Frost offers that thought in his famous essay \"The Figure That A Poem Makes\". In these deeply confusing times in our national life here are some poems that might offer just such a stay \u2013 or, as Emily Dickinson writes:\n\n\"There is no Frigate like a Book\n\nTo take us lands away\n\nNor any Coursers like a Page\n\nOf prancing Poetry \u2013\u2013\n\nThis Traverse may the poorest take\n\nWithout oppress of Toll \u2013\u2013\n\nHow frugal is the Chariot\n\nThat bears the Human soul \u2013\u2013\"\n\nHere is one of my favorite June poems by Robert Frost's friend and fellow poet, Edward Thomas\"- a poem that begins in a slow storytelling, in the \"natural speech\" that both these two poets aspired to. It then slowly morphs into a lyric, with a perfect enjambment at the end of the second quatrain as it flows into the third with all the sweetest music of an English summer afternoon, its final note lingering, undying, on the air:\n\n\"Adlestrop\n\nYes. I remember Adlestrop \u2013\u2013\n\nThe name, because one afternoon\n\nOf heat the express-train drew up there\n\nUnwontedly. It was late June.\n\nThe steam hissed. Someone cleared his throat.\n\nNo one left and no one came\n\nOn the bare platform. What I saw\n\nWas Adlestrop \u2013\u2013 only the name\n\nAnd willows, willow-herb, and grass,\n\nAnd meadowsweet, and haycocks dry,\n\nNo whit less still and lonely fair\n\nThan the high cloudlets in the sky.\n\nAnd for that minute a blackbird sang\n\nClose by, and round him, mistier,\n\nFarther and farther, all the birds\n\nOf Oxfordshire and Gloucestershire.\"\n\nAs John Keats says in his gem-like sonnet \u2013\u2013 with its deeply satisfying shift in tone from a major chord in the octet to a minor key in the sestet \u2013\u2013\n\n\"The poetry of earth is never dead:\n\nWhen all the birds are faint with the hot sun,